      "generated_answer": "The combination of a converging lens (focal length \\( f \\)) with a plane mirror in contact acts as a mirror with power equal to twice the lens power, i.e., \\( 2/f \\). A spherical concave mirror has power \\( 2/R \\), where \\( R \\) is its radius of curvature. Equating the powers gives \\( 2/f = 2/R \\), so \\( R = f \\). Thus, the system is equivalent to a concave mirror of radius \\( f \\).\n\n\\boxed{I}",
