    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The sum is \\( n = 1! + 2! + \\dots + 10! \\). For \\( k \\ge 5 \\), \\( k! \\) ends in 0, so the last digit of \\( n \\) is determined by \\( 1! + 2! + 3! + 4! = 1 + 2 + 6 + 24 = 33 \\), which ends in 3. The last digit of \\( n^n \\) is \\( 3^n \\mod 10 \\); since \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4 \\) (from \\( 1!+2!+3! = 9 \\equiv 1 \\mod 4 \\)), and powers of 3 cycle every 4, \\( 3^n \\equiv 3^1 = 3 \\pmod{10} \\).\n\n\\boxed{A}",
