    "global index": "MedMCQA_663",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following structures is not of ectodermal origin:\n\nOptions: \nA. Hunter-schreger bands\nB. Enamel spindles\nC. Enamel tufts\nD. Enamel lamellae\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Hunter-Schreger bands, enamel tufts, and enamel lamellae are structural features within enamel and are thus of ectodermal origin, as enamel is produced by ameloblasts derived from oral ectoderm. In contrast, enamel spindles are the terminal ends of dentinal tubules (odontoblast processes) that become trapped in enamel; odontoblasts originate from neural crest ectomesenchyme, which is not classified as pure ectoderm in this context. Therefore, enamel spindles are not of ectodermal origin.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
