    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Following bilateral mandibular fracture in the canine region, the following muscles will tend to pull the mandible back:\n\nOptions: \nA. Genioglossus and anterior belly of digastric\nB. Genioglossus and mylohyoid\nC. Genioglossus and thyrohyoid\nD. Genioglossus and masseter\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is B. In a bilateral mandibular fracture in the canine region, the anterior segment is detached from the rest of the mandible. The genioglossus and mylohyoid muscles, which attach to the anterior mandible, act to pull this free segment posteriorly and inferiorly. The thyrohyoid does not attach to the mandible, the masseter only elevates the posterior ramus, and while the anterior digastric also contributes to displacement, the mylohyoid is the classic broad muscle pairing with the genioglossus in this specific context.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
