      "generated_answer": "The harp's glissando sweeps across all seven strings per octave, so it can only produce sets of seven pitches (or fewer if using enharmonic duplicates). A diatonic scale (①) and the A♭ Lydian mode (②) use seven distinct notes and can be set with the pedals. The diminished seventh chord (③, G♯°⁷ in A harmonic minor) has four pitch classes but can fill all seven strings via enharmonic equivalents. A dominant seventh chord (④, D⁷) cannot avoid the E string sounding a non-chord tone. The chromatic scale (⑤) requires twelve notes, impossible on a single harp glissando.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
