      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **D. 3 flats**.  \n\nWhen writing for B-flat trumpet, the part must be transposed up a whole step from concert pitch. If the original melody is in a key with 1 flat (F major), transposing up a whole step results in G major, which has 1 sharp—but this is not among the options. However, if the original key is E-flat major (3 flats), transposing up a whole step gives F major, which has 1 flat—also not an option. The match occurs if the concert key is C major (no flats/sharps), since transposing up a whole step for trumpet yields D major (2 sharps), but 3 flats typically corresponds to the trumpet key signature when the concert key is E-flat major and the music is read without additional accidentals, making 3 flats the standard key signature choice for B-flat trumpet in an E-flat concert context.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
