    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_88",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is the most likely cause of a buzzing sound when a beginner snare drum student plays fast?\n\nOptions: \nA. The student is hammering the drumstick onto the drum.\nB. The student is playing multiple strokes on the drum.\nC. The student is dropping the drumstick onto the drum.\nD. The student is playing single strokes on the drum.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely cause of an unintended buzzing sound when a beginner plays fast is excessive tension and force, often described as \"hammering\" the stick into the drumhead, which chokes the natural rebound and causes the snare wires to vibrate irregularly. Proper technique relies on controlled rebound rather than forced, downward strokes. Dropping the stick or playing multiple strokes (buzz roll) would produce different intentional sounds or be less likely to cause this specific error.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
