    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_99",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following describes a sequence in which the second segment moves to a different tonal center than the first?\n\nOptions: \nA. Real sequence\nB. False sequence\nC. Modulating sequence\nD. Tonal sequence\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A modulating sequence is one where the pattern is transposed to a different key, meaning the second segment moves to a new tonal center.  \nIn contrast, a tonal sequence stays within the original key, and a real sequence transposes exactly without changing key.  \nThus, the correct choice here is **modulating sequence**.  \n\n\\boxed{C}",
